igher temperatures induce worse data, but at the same time increases G-Vendi , indicating higher data diversity ( Figure 6 ). As such, we treat G-Norm and G-Vendi as baselines and propose GRACE as a single score that unifies both. 2.2 The GRACE Score GRAdient Cross-validation Evaluation ( GRACE ) computes the norm of the gradients weighted under the spectrum of the normalized gradient second-moment matrix, thereby combining the advantages of G-Vendi and G-Norm . GRACE is computed solely using the student’s gradients on the teacher’s generations, and does not rely on a verifier or access to test samples. In the following, we will first define the GRACE score, and then describe its connection to leave-one-out conditional mutual information. GRACE. For a dataset 𝒟 \mathcal{D} of teacher generations containing n × m n	imes m prompt-generation pairs and a choice of hyperparameter C C , construct C C partitions of the prompts in the dataset 𝒟 \mathcal{D} , denoted { 𝒟 i } i = 1 C \{\mathcal{D}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{C} , each containing n / C n/C prompts and their generations. Let 𝒟 − i \mathcal{D}_{-i} denote the concatenation of all partitions except the partition 𝒟 i \mathcal{D}_{i} . Then, GRACE is defined as GRACE ​ ( 𝒟 ) \displaystyle	ext{GRACE}{}(\mathcal{D}) = 1 C ​ ∑ i = 1 C Tr ⁡ ( 𝚺 ^ ​ ( 𝒟 − i ) − 1 ​ 𝚺 ​ ( 𝒟 i ) ) \displaystyle= rac{1}{C}\sum_{i=1}^{C}\Tr\left(\hat{{ m{\Sigma}}}(\mathcal{D}_{-i})^{-1} m{\Sigma}(\mathcal{D}_{i})ight) (4) = 1 n ​ m ∑ i = 1 C ∑ ( 𝐱 , 𝐲 ) ∈ 𝒟 i ∥ 𝚺 ^ ( 𝒟 − i ) − 1 / 2 𝒉 ( 𝐱 , 𝐲 ) ∥ 2 , \displaystyle= rac{1}{nm}\sum_{i=1}^{C}\sum_{(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\in\mathcal{D}_{i}}\|\hat{{ m{\Sigma}}}(\mathcal{D}_{-i})^{-1/2}{ m{h}}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})\|^{2}, (5) where 𝚺 ^ ​ ( 𝒟 − i ) = 𝚺 ~ ​ ( 𝒟 − i ) + ν d ​ 𝑰 \hat{{ m{\Sigma}}}(\mathcal{D}_{-i})=	ilde{ m{\Sigma}}(\mathcal{D}_{-i})+ rac{
